WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:09.851
Now in her 17th term in Congress, House Speaker
Nancy Pelosi has been front and center all

00:00:09.851 --> 00:00:14.420
year working to advance the Biden administration’s
agenda, and her poll numbers show the scars.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.980
The formidable Speaker, like her Republican
predecessor Paul Ryan, is regarded more unfavorably

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.109
than favorably today.

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:25.429
A November 2021 Wall Street Journal poll of
registered voters put her rating at 36 percent

00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:28.939
favorable and 57 percent unfavorable.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:34.420
Gallup has tracked Pelosi’s ratings since
2003, when she became House Minority Leader.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.060
Since 2008, her unfavorable ratings have always
exceeded her favorable ones.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.739
She’s not alone.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:42.469
Most congressional leaders are more unpopular
than popular.

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.469
In Gallup’s latest question, for example,
her Republican counterpart in the Senate,

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:47.059
Mitch McConnell was more unfavorably regarded
than Pelosi.

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:51.289
Pelosi’s ability to navigate the different
factions in her party is well known.

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:56.949
But she may not be able to survive losing
the House: The most recent CNBC All-American

00:00:56.949 --> 00:01:01.920
survey found the largest GOP advantage ever
(10 points) on the question that asks which

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:18.760
party Americans would prefer to control Congress
in the next election.

